<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kurapaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kurapaty is a wooded area on the northern outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, where the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> shot and buried thousands of people between 1937 and 1941. Mass graves were uncovered in 1988, and the site became a symbol of Stalinist repression in Belarus. Estimates of the number of victims range from about 7,000 to several tens of thousands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kurapaty.jpg" alt="Kurapaty" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tobster at German Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurapaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
